      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of a Product Given the Necessary Data",
      "problem": "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-7)=2\\sqrt{3},\\,{f}^{′}(-7)=\\sqrt{3},\\,g(-7)=5\\sqrt{3}\\,{g}^{′}(-7)=-4\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=-7.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))=f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-7,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=-7} & =f(-7){g}^{′}(-7)+g(-7){f}^{′}(-7) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-4\\sqrt{3})+5\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
