      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of a Product Given the Necessary Data",
      "problem": "For the function $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(e)=1,\\,{f}^{′}(e)=\\frac{1}{e},\\,g(e)={e}^{2},\\,{g}^{′}(e)=2e\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=e.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x)) & =f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=e$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=e} & =f(e){g}^{′}(e)+g(e){f}^{′}(e) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2e+{e}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{e} \\\\  & =3e.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
