      "problem": "For the function $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(4)=-3,\\,{f}^{′}(4)=-1,\\,g(4)=2,\\,{g}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=4.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x)) & =f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=4$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=4} & =f(4){g}^{′}(4)+g(4){f}^{′}(4) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
