      "problem": "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(0)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(0)=2,\\,g(0)=1,\\,{g}^{′}(0)=2\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})$ at $x=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-f(x){g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=0$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(0)g(0)-f(0){g}^{′}(0)}{{g}^{2}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
