    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-341013",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d2b7d79a-cd1a-583f-be7a-d568bc8e32a3",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient Given the Necessary Data",
      "problem": "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(4)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(4)=\\sqrt{2},\\,g(4)=\\sqrt{2},\\,{g}^{′}(4)=-1\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})$ at $x=4.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=4}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-f(x){g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=4,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=4} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(4)g(4)-f(4){g}^{′}(4)}{(g(4))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-2\\cdot (-1)}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
