      "problem": "For the functions $f(x)$ and $p(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-3)=-6,\\,{f}^{′}(-3)=2,\\,p(-3)=-5,\\,{p}^{′}(-3)=-3\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+p(x))$ at $x=-3.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+p(x))={f}^{′}(x)+{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-3$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+p(x))|}_{x=-3} & ={f}^{′}(-3)+{p}^{′}(-3) \\\\  & =2+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
