      "math_academy_id": "q-341042",
      "knowledge_point_id": "93eb24a9-f358-50cf-b447-c394864843f9",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Combination of Functions Given the Necessary Data",
      "problem": "For the functions $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nu(4)=1,\\,{u}^{′}(4)=6,\\,v(4)=-2,\\,{v}^{′}(4)=2\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))$ at $x=4.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))|}_{x=4}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{′}(x)+3{v}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=4$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))|}_{x=4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{′}(4)+3{v}^{′}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6)+3(2) \\\\  & =3+6 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
