    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-341468",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f1d07a6d-3994-5620-a756-ee6e2c0a8de6",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of the Product or Quotient of a Composition of Functions Given the Necessary Data",
      "problem": "The values of functions $h(t)$ and $z(t)$ and their derivatives at $t=4$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $h(t)$ | $z(t)$ | ${h}^{′}(t)$ | ${z}^{′}(t)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nIf $f(t)=\\frac{2h(t)-5}{1-z(t)},$ compute ${f}^{′}(4).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2h(t)-5$ and $v=1-z(t).$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2{h}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{z}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2{h}^{′}(t)(1-z(t))-(2h(t)-5)(-{z}^{′}(t))}{(1-z(t))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAt $t=4,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{2{h}^{′}(4)(1-z(4))-(2h(4)-5)(-{z}^{′}(4))}{(1-z(4))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(3)(1-(-2))-(2(1)-5)(-1)}{(1-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(3)(3)-(-3)(-1)}{(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18-3}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
