    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-341450",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f1d07a6d-3994-5620-a756-ee6e2c0a8de6",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of the Product or Quotient of a Composition of Functions Given the Necessary Data",
      "problem": "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=2$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n\nIf $h(x)=(-2f(x)-3)(g(x)+1),$ find the value of ${h}^{′}(2).$\n\n${h}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Explanation:\n\nThe product rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(uv)=u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-2f(x)-3$ and $v=g(x)+1.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =-2{f}^{′}(x)(g(x)+1)+(-2f(x)-3){g}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =-2{f}^{′}(2)(g(2)+1)+(-2f(2)-3){g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-2(2)(-3+1)+(-2(4)-3)(5) \\\\  & =-4(-2)+(-8-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =8+(-11)(5) \\\\  & =8-55 \\\\  & =-47.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-47"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "-47"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "moderate",
      "provenance": {
