    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-105495",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f1d07a6d-3994-5620-a756-ee6e2c0a8de6",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of the Product or Quotient of a Composition of Functions Given the Necessary Data",
      "problem": "For the functions $p(x)$ and $q(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\np(10)=-1,\\,{p}^{′}(10)=-2,\\,q(10)=2,\\,{q}^{′}(10)=1\n$$\n\nIf $z(x)=(2p(x)+q(x))(2-p(x))$, compute ${z}^{′}(10).$",
      "worked_solution": "The product rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(uv)=u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2p(x)+q(x)$ and $v=2-p(x).$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{p}^{′}(x)+{q}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}(x) & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(2p(x)+q(x))(-{p}^{′}(x))+(2-p(x))(2{p}^{′}(x)+{q}^{′}(x)) \\\\  & =-{p}^{′}(x)(2p(x)+q(x))+(2{p}^{′}(x)+{q}^{′}(x))(2-p(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=10,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}(10) & =-{p}^{′}(10)(2p(10)+q(10))+(2{p}^{′}(10)+{q}^{′}(10))(2-p(10)) \\\\  & =-(-2)(2(-1)+2)+(2(-2)+1)(2-(-1)) \\\\  & =2(-2+2)-3(2+1) \\\\  & =0-9 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
