    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-4961",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f1d07a6d-3994-5620-a756-ee6e2c0a8de6",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of the Product or Quotient of a Composition of Functions Given the Necessary Data",
      "problem": "The values of functions $h(x)$ and $z(x)$ and their derivatives at $t=2$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $h(t)$ | $z(t)$ | ${h}^{′}(t)$ | ${z}^{′}(t)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $8$ | $-3$ | $4$ |\n\nIf $f(t)=\\frac{1-h(t)}{z(t)},$ compute ${f}^{′}(2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-h(t)$ and $v=z(t).$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-{h}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={z}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-{h}^{′}(t))\\cdot z(t)-(1-h(t))\\cdot {z}^{′}(t)}{(z(t))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-z(t){h}^{′}(t)-{z}^{′}(t)(1-h(t))}{{z}^{2}(t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{-z(2){h}^{′}(2)-{z}^{′}(2)(1-h(2))}{{z}^{2}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\cdot (-3)-4\\cdot (1-5)}{{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24+16}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
