      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-6154",
      "knowledge_point_id": "93eb24a9-f358-50cf-b447-c394864843f9",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Combination of Functions Given the Necessary Data",
      "problem": "The values of functions $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-3$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $p(x)$ | $q(x)$ | $p'(x)$ | $q'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $8$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n\nCompute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\big( p(x) + 5q(x) \\big)$ at $x=-3.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( p(x) + 5q(x) \\right) = p'(x) + 5q'(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-3$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(p(x)+5q(x))|}_{x=-3} & ={p}^{′}(-3)+5{q}^{′}(-3) \\\\  & =0+5\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
